1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,280
Hell has an address.

2
00:00:02,110 --> 00:00:06,150
It's a valley on the edge of old
Jerusalem, just below the city walls.

3
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:08,637
You can walk through it today.

4
00:00:09,137 --> 00:00:10,937
In summer, it hosts concerts.

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,980
In 1987, Bob Dylan played there.

6
00:00:15,584 --> 00:00:18,264
In Hebrew, it's Ge Hinnom,
the Valley of Hinnom.

7
00:00:19,115 --> 00:00:22,315
In the Greek of the New Testament,
that became Gehenna.

8
00:00:22,935 --> 00:00:25,555
In the Arabic of the Quran,
very probably, Jahannam.

9
00:00:26,829 --> 00:00:31,349
Most English translations
turn both of those words into one: hell.

10
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:35,222
Over about two thousand years,
for billions of people,

11
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:37,842
the name of that valley
came to mean one thing:

12
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:39,922
a place of fire for the wicked.

13
00:00:40,653 --> 00:00:43,453
So how does a valley
you can walk through become hell?

14
00:00:44,498 --> 00:00:46,238
Here's the strange part.

15
00:00:47,058 --> 00:00:48,578
In the older books of the Bible,

16
00:00:48,658 --> 00:00:51,798
the people living next to that valley
don't have a hell at all.

17
00:00:52,361 --> 00:00:54,901
Their scriptures do have a place
for the dead.

18
00:00:55,301 --> 00:00:56,501
It's called Sheol.

19
00:00:57,345 --> 00:00:59,345
It's dark. It's silent.

20
00:00:59,605 --> 00:01:01,185
And everyone goes there.

21
00:01:01,668 --> 00:01:03,548
The Bible even shows you.

22
00:01:03,728 --> 00:01:08,588
King Saul, abandoned by God, has
a medium call up the dead prophet Samuel.

23
00:01:09,254 --> 00:01:11,474
Samuel is annoyed at being disturbed.

24
00:01:12,334 --> 00:01:16,274
Then he tells the king: tomorrow,
you and your sons will be with me.

25
00:01:16,797 --> 00:01:20,757
The prophet,
and the king God rejected. Same place.

26
00:01:21,524 --> 00:01:24,404
Nobody is rewarded. Nobody is punished.

27
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:28,963
In ancient Mesopotamia,
how you fared among the dead

28
00:01:29,193 --> 00:01:31,283
had little to do with how you'd behaved.

29
00:01:32,003 --> 00:01:35,923
It depended on how many sons you had,
because sons brought offerings.

30
00:01:36,769 --> 00:01:38,969
A Sumerian poem spells it out.

31
00:01:39,629 --> 00:01:41,509
One son, and you sit and weep.

32
00:01:41,889 --> 00:01:45,189
Seven sons, and you get a throne
among the junior gods.

33
00:01:45,957 --> 00:01:48,537
So what happened to bad people
when they died?

34
00:01:49,296 --> 00:01:52,176
Nothing special.
Being bad wasn't what counted.

35
00:01:53,956 --> 00:01:57,556
Hell wasn't there from the start.
It had to be built.

36
00:01:57,935 --> 00:02:00,135
And nobody built it all at once.

37
00:02:00,655 --> 00:02:03,955
You can follow it through five parts,
over thousands of years.

38
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:07,785
Whether any of it is true,
no historian can tell you.

39
00:02:07,985 --> 00:02:11,645
How much each culture borrowed
from the last is still debated.

40
00:02:12,525 --> 00:02:14,205
But the parts are there to see.

41
00:02:16,615 --> 00:02:18,055
Part one is a court.

42
00:02:18,715 --> 00:02:21,795
Egypt,
about three and a half thousand years ago.

43
00:02:22,374 --> 00:02:24,774
You die,
and you're brought before the gods.

44
00:02:25,474 --> 00:02:28,394
You swear you haven't committed
any of forty-two sins.

45
00:02:28,849 --> 00:02:31,349
Then your heart is weighed
against a feather.

46
00:02:31,674 --> 00:02:33,354
If it fails, a monster called Ammit

47
00:02:33,464 --> 00:02:37,574
is waiting, part crocodile,
part lion, part hippo.

48
00:02:38,274 --> 00:02:40,854
It eats the heart,
and your afterlife is over.

49
00:02:41,415 --> 00:02:45,195
It's one of the oldest versions
of an idea we now take for granted:

50
00:02:45,555 --> 00:02:48,015
how you live decides what happens next.

51
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,440
There was just one loophole.

52
00:02:51,092 --> 00:02:53,692
Another spell spoke
directly to your heart,

53
00:02:53,772 --> 00:02:56,432
and begged it not to testify against you.

54
00:02:56,906 --> 00:02:58,886
You could have it written on your scroll.

55
00:02:59,546 --> 00:03:02,886
A scroll could cost
perhaps half a labourer's yearly pay.

56
00:03:03,253 --> 00:03:05,273
So one of the first
moral courts in history

57
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:07,793
already came with a paid workaround.

58
00:03:08,661 --> 00:03:10,321
What it didn't have was forever.

59
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:13,701
Egyptian texts also
describe lakes of fire,

60
00:03:13,781 --> 00:03:16,161
but the fire was there to destroy you.

61
00:03:16,741 --> 00:03:18,781
The worst fate was to stop existing.

62
00:03:21,511 --> 00:03:23,091
Part two is a sentence.

63
00:03:24,031 --> 00:03:26,411
Athens, around 380 BC.

64
00:03:26,491 --> 00:03:29,071
Plato has Socrates tell a story.

65
00:03:29,631 --> 00:03:33,951
After death, a judge inspects the soul,
stripped of every title and honour,

66
00:03:34,031 --> 00:03:38,451
and sends the bad to Tartarus,
labelled curable or incurable.

67
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:41,330
The curable are improved by punishment.

68
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:45,310
The incurable suffer forever,
as a warning to others.

69
00:03:45,674 --> 00:03:47,374
And Plato gives an example.

70
00:03:47,454 --> 00:03:48,674
Not a legend.

71
00:03:48,904 --> 00:03:54,314
Archelaus, a real king of Macedon, said
to have murdered his way to the throne.

72
00:03:55,012 --> 00:04:00,472
Keep that in mind. A real, powerful man,
named in a story about who gets punished.

73
00:04:03,202 --> 00:04:05,542
Part three is the one that matters most.

74
00:04:06,042 --> 00:04:07,242
It's a motive.

75
00:04:07,865 --> 00:04:10,425
Remember, those older books had no hell.

76
00:04:10,845 --> 00:04:13,825
Justice was supposed to be done here,
in this life.

77
00:04:14,470 --> 00:04:16,830
The Book of Deuteronomy
spells out the deal.

78
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:18,470
Obey, and you're blessed.

79
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:21,850
Disobey, and the curses come for you.

80
00:04:22,356 --> 00:04:25,636
The trouble with that deal
is that anyone can check it.

81
00:04:26,228 --> 00:04:28,268
The Book of Ecclesiastes did.

82
00:04:28,888 --> 00:04:31,708
There are just people, it says,
who get what the wicked deserve,

83
00:04:31,938 --> 00:04:34,768
and wicked people
who get what the just deserve.

84
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:39,642
Some people do terrible things,
and die old, rich and comfortable.

85
00:04:40,953 --> 00:04:44,953
So, more than two thousand years ago,
someone wrote down an answer.

86
00:04:45,572 --> 00:04:49,772
It's in a text called First Enoch,
which most Bibles don't include.

87
00:04:50,652 --> 00:04:53,992
An angel shows Enoch
the hollow places where the dead wait.

88
00:04:54,694 --> 00:04:56,514
There's a section for the righteous.

89
00:04:57,304 --> 00:05:00,784
And there's a section for sinners.
But not all sinners.

90
00:05:01,583 --> 00:05:03,123
It's for those who die, and, quote,

91
00:05:03,353 --> 00:05:06,783
"judgement has not been
executed on them in their lifetime".

92
00:05:07,674 --> 00:05:09,394
Listen to that again.

93
00:05:10,576 --> 00:05:12,076
Not a place for sinners.

94
00:05:12,256 --> 00:05:15,036
A place for sinners who got away with it.

95
00:05:17,086 --> 00:05:19,066
Then the deal broke completely.

96
00:05:19,913 --> 00:05:25,373
In 167 BC, Antiochus the Fourth,
the Greek king ruling over Judea,

97
00:05:25,453 --> 00:05:27,873
moved to crush traditional Jewish worship.

98
00:05:28,521 --> 00:05:31,581
Now it wasn't just that good people
failed to prosper.

99
00:05:32,261 --> 00:05:34,401
They were being killed
for keeping the law.

100
00:05:35,062 --> 00:05:38,562
The Second Book of Maccabees
tells of a mother and seven sons,

101
00:05:38,642 --> 00:05:42,102
killed one after another,
for refusing to eat pork.

102
00:05:42,795 --> 00:05:47,215
One son, dying, tells the king:
you will have no resurrection to life.

103
00:05:47,926 --> 00:05:50,486
Under the old deal,
none of this made sense.

104
00:05:51,426 --> 00:05:54,386
Either God had failed them,
or the story wasn't over.

105
00:05:55,378 --> 00:05:58,238
In those same years,
the Book of Daniel was finished.

106
00:05:58,878 --> 00:06:00,538
Its last chapter says:

107
00:06:01,215 --> 00:06:04,395
"Many of them that sleep
in the dust of the earth shall awake,

108
00:06:04,635 --> 00:06:08,875
some to everlasting life, and some
to shame and everlasting contempt."

109
00:06:09,588 --> 00:06:11,388
The dead were no longer equal.

110
00:06:11,488 --> 00:06:13,648
And the verdict had no end.

111
00:06:16,528 --> 00:06:18,228
Part four is the location.

112
00:06:19,238 --> 00:06:21,358
Back to that valley below Jerusalem.

113
00:06:22,218 --> 00:06:23,418
Why that one?

114
00:06:23,858 --> 00:06:26,958
Because it already had
the worst reputation in the country.

115
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:30,720
Jeremiah accuses people
of building a shrine there,

116
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,780
"to burn their sons and their daughters
in the fire".

117
00:06:34,432 --> 00:06:37,372
Archaeology hasn't
confirmed those burnings.

118
00:06:37,452 --> 00:06:40,032
But the reputation didn't need proof.

119
00:06:40,459 --> 00:06:43,839
And then Jeremiah makes a threat,
about that same spot.

120
00:06:44,419 --> 00:06:46,539
It will be called the Valley of Slaughter,

121
00:06:46,819 --> 00:06:49,879
and the dead will be buried there
until there's no room left.

122
00:06:50,584 --> 00:06:53,524
The place of the crime
becomes the place of the punishment.

123
00:06:55,244 --> 00:06:58,164
The very last verse of Isaiah
imagines people going out

124
00:06:58,394 --> 00:07:02,124
to look at the corpses of the rebels:
"their worm shall not die,

125
00:07:02,204 --> 00:07:04,384
neither shall their fire be quenched".

126
00:07:04,554 --> 00:07:08,754
And First Enoch describes
an accursed valley beside a holy mountain,

127
00:07:09,034 --> 00:07:11,347
"for those who are accursed for ever".

128
00:07:11,648 --> 00:07:14,488
Fire. Corpses. A curse.

129
00:07:14,628 --> 00:07:17,068
And a real place, just outside town.

130
00:07:18,257 --> 00:07:21,497
By the time of Jesus,
Gehenna meant more than a valley.

131
00:07:22,257 --> 00:07:25,357
It was the name for the place
where God punishes the wicked.

132
00:07:26,099 --> 00:07:29,039
In the Gospel of Mark,
he warns of Gehenna,

133
00:07:29,119 --> 00:07:32,519
and quotes that line from Isaiah
about the worm and the fire.

134
00:07:33,171 --> 00:07:36,031
The word appears twelve times
in the New Testament,

135
00:07:36,151 --> 00:07:38,391
eleven of them in the words of Jesus.

136
00:07:39,155 --> 00:07:43,455
You may have heard that Gehenna was the
city's rubbish dump, always burning.

137
00:07:44,094 --> 00:07:45,534
It's a neat explanation.

138
00:07:45,694 --> 00:07:49,594
But it's first found around 1200 AD,
in a rabbi's commentary,

139
00:07:49,824 --> 00:07:53,074
and no direct evidence
for it has turned up.

140
00:07:54,221 --> 00:07:57,541
The word hell itself
comes from somewhere else entirely.

141
00:07:57,921 --> 00:08:01,661
It's an old Germanic word, meaning
something like the concealed place.

142
00:08:01,921 --> 00:08:03,681
A world of the dead.

143
00:08:04,081 --> 00:08:05,921
There's no fire in the word.

144
00:08:06,233 --> 00:08:10,053
Then the King James Bible used it
for Gehenna, for Greek Hades,

145
00:08:10,133 --> 00:08:13,153
for Tartarus,
and about half the time, for Sheol.

146
00:08:13,553 --> 00:08:16,153
So when Jesus warns of hell
in an English Bible,

147
00:08:16,233 --> 00:08:19,113
the word underneath is usually Gehenna.

148
00:08:19,533 --> 00:08:20,853
The name of a valley.

149
00:08:23,083 --> 00:08:24,743
Part five is the pictures.

150
00:08:25,693 --> 00:08:30,293
Picture hell. You probably see torture,
demons, and the devil in charge.

151
00:08:31,482 --> 00:08:35,002
But in the Book of Revelation,
the devil isn't running the place.

152
00:08:35,322 --> 00:08:36,722
He's thrown in.

153
00:08:37,297 --> 00:08:40,797
The oldest detailed Christian tour of hell
we have is another book,

154
00:08:40,877 --> 00:08:45,237
the Apocalypse of Peter,
written between 100 and 150 AD.

155
00:08:46,374 --> 00:08:47,834
Blasphemers hang by their tongues.

156
00:08:48,064 --> 00:08:51,654
Usurers stand to their knees in filth.

157
00:08:52,194 --> 00:08:54,094
Persecutors burn up to the waist.

158
00:08:54,794 --> 00:08:57,134
It came very close to being in the Bible.

159
00:08:57,877 --> 00:09:02,497
One of the oldest lists of Christian
scripture accepts it alongside Revelation,

160
00:09:02,777 --> 00:09:05,637
while admitting some didn't
want it heard in church.

161
00:09:06,442 --> 00:09:08,622
In the end, it stayed out of the Bible.

162
00:09:09,002 --> 00:09:10,642
Its pictures lived on.

163
00:09:11,298 --> 00:09:13,498
They passed into a later tour of hell,

164
00:09:13,578 --> 00:09:16,738
the Apocalypse of Paul,
which Dante probably knew.

165
00:09:17,324 --> 00:09:21,584
In his Inferno, Pope Nicholas the Third
is stuck head down in a hole.

166
00:09:22,178 --> 00:09:24,398
He hears someone coming, and calls out:

167
00:09:24,478 --> 00:09:26,898
"Are you already standing there,
Boniface?"

168
00:09:27,522 --> 00:09:29,642
The poem is set in 1300.

169
00:09:30,442 --> 00:09:33,322
Boniface the Eighth was alive,
and he was the pope.

170
00:09:33,998 --> 00:09:36,218
Dante had reserved his place.

171
00:09:38,731 --> 00:09:41,251
Now look at who these hells
were built for.

172
00:09:41,814 --> 00:09:42,974
Plato's murderous king.

173
00:09:43,204 --> 00:09:46,074
Enoch's sinners who died unpunished.

174
00:09:46,394 --> 00:09:48,234
The persecutors of the righteous.

175
00:09:48,654 --> 00:09:50,174
Dante's living pope.

176
00:09:50,810 --> 00:09:53,170
Hell isn't just a theory about death.

177
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:55,890
It's also a complaint about life.

178
00:09:57,204 --> 00:10:00,504
Somebody did something terrible,
and nothing happened to them.

179
00:10:00,804 --> 00:10:03,684
Hell is where the story goes to fix that.

180
00:10:03,978 --> 00:10:06,298
That doesn't tell
you whether hell is real.

181
00:10:06,898 --> 00:10:10,118
Where an idea comes from
can't prove it or disprove it.

182
00:10:10,625 --> 00:10:13,225
It only tells you why people needed it.

183
00:10:16,185 --> 00:10:18,225
But a list like that has a problem.

184
00:10:18,765 --> 00:10:20,425
It doesn't stop at tyrants.

185
00:10:21,147 --> 00:10:23,327
Look again at the Apocalypse of Peter.

186
00:10:24,407 --> 00:10:28,607
On the same list as the persecutors,
there are women hanging by their hair,

187
00:10:28,687 --> 00:10:30,967
for plaiting it to lure men into adultery.

188
00:10:31,869 --> 00:10:34,249
A place imagined
for people who got away with murder

189
00:10:34,479 --> 00:10:36,789
also had room for your neighbour.

190
00:10:37,105 --> 00:10:40,265
And a threat that covers your neighbour
also covers you.

191
00:10:42,515 --> 00:10:46,115
A little over half of American adults
say they believe in hell.

192
00:10:46,775 --> 00:10:48,435
But the picture keeps shifting.

193
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:53,780
In 1999, Pope John Paul the Second
told a public audience:

194
00:10:54,180 --> 00:10:57,020
"Rather than a place,
hell indicates the state of those

195
00:10:57,250 --> 00:11:00,540
who freely and definitively
separate themselves from God."

196
00:11:01,184 --> 00:11:02,764
Rather than a place.

197
00:11:04,145 --> 00:11:05,945
It wasn't a change of doctrine.

198
00:11:06,545 --> 00:11:10,985
But after two thousand years, a pope
was describing hell without an address.

199
00:11:12,131 --> 00:11:13,631
The valley still has one.

200
00:11:16,672 --> 00:11:19,152
So where did the idea of hell come from?

201
00:11:19,776 --> 00:11:22,996
A court in Egypt. A sentence in Greece.

202
00:11:23,336 --> 00:11:25,096
A motive in Judea.

203
00:11:25,216 --> 00:11:26,996
A valley outside Jerusalem.

204
00:11:27,556 --> 00:11:30,536
And pictures from a book
that didn't make it into the Bible.

205
00:11:31,005 --> 00:11:35,005
And holding them together, one old
feeling: somebody got away with it.

206
00:11:37,195 --> 00:11:39,075
So here's a question for you.

207
00:11:39,675 --> 00:11:41,835
If there's no court after death,
is it up to us

208
00:11:42,065 --> 00:11:44,335
to make sure nobody gets away with it?

209
00:11:44,566 --> 00:11:48,006
Or is some unfairness
just something we have to live with?

210
00:11:48,545 --> 00:11:49,855
Let me know in the comments.
